    "q-95169": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Given that $h(x,y,z)=xyz,$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates.",
      "worked_solution": "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $h(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂h}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂h}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂h}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=yz,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂y}=xz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =rz\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta } & =-(rz\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}z{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}z({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={r}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
